{"ts": "2026-08-17T11:21:41Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 18, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "---\n# Spec: Keep my tasks when the server restarts\n\nTaiga #5\n\n## Triage\n\n**Feature** \u2014 net-new capability, but purely additive at the persistence\nlayer. Touches only `backend/app/store.py`, backend test setup, and\n`.gitignore`. No route changes, no request/response shape changes, no\nfrontend changes \u2014 every existing endpoint already goes through\n`TaskStore`, so persistence is transparent underneath it. Single\narchitectural layer (data/store), so this stays one spec, not split into a\nsequence.\n\n## Goal\n\nA task created (or completed, retitled, reprioritized, deleted) survives a\nserver restart \u2014 nothing is lost just because the process stopped.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. Given the server is running, when a task is created via\n   `POST /api/tasks`, then the full task list is written to the data file\n   before the response is returned, so reading the file afterward reflects\n   the new task.\n2. Given step 1, and any of `POST /api/tasks/{id}/done`,\n   `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}`, `PATCH /api/tasks/{id}/priority`, or\n   `DELETE /api/tasks/{id}` \u2014 each of these also writes the full task list\n   to disk synchronously as part of the request, not deferred or batched.\n   A restart immediately after any one of these does not lose that change.\n3. Given a data file on disk containing previously-saved tasks, when the\n   server starts, then those tasks are loaded into the store with `id`,\n   `title`, `done`, and `priority` all intact, and `GET /api/tasks` returns\n   them immediately, with no client action required.\n4. Given tasks were loaded from disk with the highest id being `N`, when a\n   new task is created, then its id is `N + 1` \u2014 the id counter picks up\n   from the persisted data, never restarting at 1 and never colliding with\n   a loaded id.\n5. Given the data file does not exist (first run, or it was deleted), when\n   the server starts, then the store starts empty, no error is raised, and\n   the server serves requests normally \u2014 identical to today's behaviour\n   before this feature existed.\n6. Given the data file exists but is not valid JSON, or is valid JSON that\n   doesn't match the expected shape (e.g. a JSON object instead of an\n   array, an empty file, or an array of the wrong item shape), when the\n   server starts, then the store starts empty, no exception propagates out\n   of startup, and the server serves requests normally.\n7. Given the configured data directory doesn't exist yet, when the first\n   write happens, then the directory is created automatically rather than\n   raising `FileNotFoundError`.\n8. Backend tests remain hermetic: they never read or write the real data\n   file used by a locally-running server, and each test still starts from\n   an empty store, matching today's `_clean_store` autouse fixture\n   behaviour.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- No new HTTP endpoint for export/import/backup of the data file \u2014 this is\n  an internal implementation detail, not a new API surface.\n- No database. A JSON file is still not a database; introducing one is\n  explicitly called out in `backend/app/store.py`'s own docstring as \"a\n  decision, not an implementation detail,\" and this spec doesn't make that\n  decision.\n- No multi-process or multi-worker file locking. This app runs as a single\n  `uvicorn` process today (`--reload` included); concurrent writers to the\n  same file are not a scenario this app creates.\n- No partial recovery of a corrupt file (e.g. salvaging the valid entries\n  out of a partially-truncated JSON array). \"Corrupt \u2192 start empty\" is the\n  entire recovery story per the request; anything more is out of scope.\n- No versioning/backup of previous file contents (no `.bak` rotation).\n- No frontend change \u2014 the UI already just calls the existing endpoints and\n  reflects whatever the backend returns.\n- No change to `backend/app/models.py` \u2014 the `Task` shape on the wire is\n  unchanged; only its at-rest serialization is new.\n\n## Affected areas\n\nBackend:\n- `backend/app/store.py` \u2014 `TaskStore.__init__` gains a data-file path\n  (resolved from an environment variable with a fixed default, see\n  decision below) and, on construction, attempts to load existing tasks\n  from that path. Every mutating method \u2014 `add` (line ~19), `set_done`\n  (~30), `set_priority` (~35), `update_title` (~40), `delete` (~45), and\n  `reset` (~49, used by tests) \u2014 writes the full current task list to disk\n  after mutating in-memory state. `_next_id` initializes to\n  `max(loaded task ids, default=0) + 1` instead of always `1`.\n- `backend/app/main.py` \u2014 no changes expected. Every route already goes\n  through `store`; persistence is invisible to the route layer.\n- `backend/data/` (new directory, not currently present) \u2014 default home\n  for `tasks.json`. Created on first write if missing (AC7).\n- `backend/tests/conftest.py` \u2014 set the data-file env var to a `pytest`\n  tmp path **before** `from app.main import app` runs (line 9), since that\n  import is what constructs the module-level `store` singleton and its\n  path is fixed at construction time. Without this, tests would load from\n  and write to the real data file.\n- `backend/tests/test_persistence.py` (new file, since this concern isn't\n  a single route \u2014 no natural `test_<route>.py` fit) \u2014 covers: state\n  surviving across two `TaskStore` instances pointed at the same tmp path\n  (simulating a restart), missing file starts empty, corrupt/malformed\n  file starts empty, id counter continuing correctly after reload, and\n  directory auto-creation.\n- `.gitignore` \u2014 add `backend/data/` so persisted task data is never\n  committed.\n\nNot affected: `frontend/` (entire directory), `backend/app/models.py`.\n\n## Decisions made here (not left open)\n\n1. **Data file location:** `backend/data/tasks.json`, resolved relative to\n   `store.py`'s own location (`Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent /\n   \"data\" / \"tasks.json\"`) rather than the process's current working\n   directory \u2014 so it resolves the same way whether uvicorn is started from\n   `backend/` (per this project's README) or elsewhere.\n2. **Override mechanism:** an environment variable (e.g. `TASKS_DATA_FILE`)\n   overrides the default path when set. This is the hook `conftest.py`\n   uses to redirect tests to an isolated tmp path \u2014 without it, tests and\n   the real running server would contend for the same file.\n3. **Write strategy:** synchronous, full-snapshot write on every mutation\n   (serialize the whole task list to JSON and write it), matching this\n   store's already-small scale (in-memory dict, no batching elsewhere).\n   Chosen over a write-ahead/append log as the simplest thing that\n   satisfies the acceptance criteria \u2014 this store has never optimized for\n   write volume and shouldn't start here.\n4. **Corrupt-file handling:** any exception raised while reading or\n   parsing the file at startup (`FileNotFoundError`, `json.JSONDecodeError`,\n   or a shape mismatch such as the parsed value not being a list of\n   objects with the expected keys) is caught at load time and treated\n   identically to \"no file\" \u2014 start empty. This is one code path, not two,\n   since the request draws no distinction between \"missing\" and \"corrupt.\"\n\n## Edge cases\n\n- Missing file on startup \u2192 start empty (AC5).\n- Corrupt/unparseable JSON on startup \u2192 start empty (AC6).\n- Valid JSON but wrong top-level shape (object instead of array) \u2192 treated\n  as corrupt, start empty.\n- Valid JSON array but entries missing required fields or with the wrong\n  types \u2192 treated as corrupt, start empty (no per-entry salvage, per\n  non-goals).\n- Empty file (0 bytes) \u2192 invalid JSON, treated as corrupt, start empty.", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01GQ2KB31t5C5t4Pj1JMdBah", "session_id": "e86ea192-3307-4635-a1ac-17336a1dfdd0"}}
